---
title: "Queue latency, Q3: the regional readout"
subtitle: "Three regions, two release channels, thirteen weeks"
author: "Platform analytics"
date: 2026-10-06
description: "A quarterly latency readout: what p95 did in each region, whether the canary channel is still paying a penalty, and what the incident in week 33 cost us."
toc: true
---

This is the quarterly readout for the request queue. It answers the three questions
the platform review actually asks: whether latency moved, whether the canary release
channel is still slower than stable, and what the week-33 incident did to the numbers.

The cleaning and the descriptive work are in Python, because that is where the
loading and reshaping is comfortable. The model is fitted in R, because that is where
the regression idiom and the tidy coefficient table live. Both languages read the same
committed CSV and both run in this one document, so the figures below are generated by
the report rather than pasted into it.

::: {.callout-note}
## The data here is simulated

`data/latency.csv` is generated from a fixed seed, not scraped from a real service, so
this report can live in a public repository and still be re-run by anyone who clones it.
The generating process is written out in full in [Methods](methods.tmd). The *analysis*
is the real thing: nothing below is hand-tuned to produce a tidy answer.
:::

## What we measured {#sec-data}

The queue is served from three regions, and every release goes out on two channels: the
`stable` channel that carries the great majority of traffic, and the `canary` channel that
takes roughly a tenth of it a few days ahead. We hold ourselves to two numbers.

| Objective | Target | Measured as |
|-----------|--------|-------------|
| Tail latency | p95 under 150 ms | The 95th percentile of queue time, per region-week |
| Error budget | Under 0.25% of requests | Failed dequeues over total requests, per region-week |

: The two service objectives this readout is written against. {#tbl-slo}

Both objectives are evaluated per region-week, which is also the grain of the dataset:
thirteen ISO weeks (27 through 39), three regions, two channels, so 78 rows.

```{python}
#| label: setup
#| echo: false

import matplotlib
import matplotlib.pyplot as plt
import pandas as pd
from IPython.display import HTML, display

# Taliesin renders every inline matplotlib figure TWICE — once with the light theme's
# foreground, once with the dark theme's — and the page swaps them when the reader
# toggles the theme, so `INK` below is only the fallback for anything the recolour does
# not reach. Data colours are never touched, so those DO have to read on both themes:
# three mid-chroma hues, no near-white and no near-black.
INK = "#7d7d7d"
REGION_COLOUR = {"eu-west": "#3b82c4", "us-east": "#c4713b", "ap-south": "#5a9e6f"}

matplotlib.rcParams.update({
    "figure.facecolor": "none",
    "axes.facecolor": "none",
    "savefig.facecolor": "none",
    "savefig.transparent": True,
    "text.color": INK,
    "axes.labelcolor": INK,
    "axes.edgecolor": INK,
    "xtick.color": INK,
    "ytick.color": INK,
    "axes.spines.top": False,
    "axes.spines.right": False,
    "axes.grid": True,
    "grid.color": INK,
    "grid.alpha": 0.22,
    "grid.linewidth": 0.6,
    "font.size": 9,
    "figure.dpi": 150,
})

INCIDENT_WEEK = 33
INCIDENT_REGION = "ap-south"

latency = pd.read_csv("data/latency.csv")
incident = (latency.week == INCIDENT_WEEK) & (latency.region == INCIDENT_REGION)
clean = latency[~incident]
```

```{python}
#| label: tbl-coverage
#| tbl-cap: "Quarter totals by region. Medians are taken over the thirteen region-weeks; the p95 column is the median of the weekly p95, not a p95 of p95s."
#| echo: false

coverage = (
    clean.groupby("region")
    .agg(
        weeks=("week", "nunique"),
        requests_M=("requests_k", lambda s: round(s.sum() / 1000, 1)),
        p50_ms=("p50_ms", "median"),
        p95_ms=("p95_ms", "median"),
        errors=("errors", "sum"),
    )
    .reset_index()
    .rename(columns={
        "region": "Region",
        "weeks": "Weeks",
        "requests_M": "Requests (M)",
        "p50_ms": "p50 (ms)",
        "p95_ms": "p95 (ms)",
        "errors": "Errors",
    })
)

# `coverage` on its own line would render pandas' own repr: a `border="1"` table
# tagged `class="dataframe"`, carrying a `<style scoped>` block (a dead attribute
# in every current browser) and a row-index column. `to_html` with the index and
# the border off emits plain markup the page's own table styling can reach.
display(HTML(coverage.to_html(index=False, border=0)))
```

@tbl-coverage is computed with the week-33 incident rows dropped, which is the only
exclusion applied anywhere in this readout. Two things are already visible in it.
`ap-south` runs slower than the other two regions at both percentiles, and it is also the
smallest by volume, so its weekly numbers are the noisiest. Every regional comparison
below has to survive that.

## How the tail moved {#sec-trend}

```{python}
#| label: fig-p95
#| fig-cap: "Weekly p95 by region. Stable is solid, canary dashed. The dotted rule is the 150 ms objective from @tbl-slo; the shaded band is the week-33 incident in ap-south, excluded from every figure that follows."
#| echo: false

fig, ax = plt.subplots(figsize=(7.2, 3.4))

for region, colour in REGION_COLOUR.items():
    for channel, style in (("stable", "-"), ("canary", "--")):
        series = latency[(latency.region == region) & (latency.channel == channel)]
        ax.plot(
            series.week,
            series.p95_ms,
            style,
            color=colour,
            linewidth=1.6 if channel == "stable" else 1.1,
            label=f"{region} · {channel}",
        )

ax.axhline(150, linestyle=":", color=INK, linewidth=1.0)
ax.axvspan(INCIDENT_WEEK - 0.4, INCIDENT_WEEK + 0.4, color=INK, alpha=0.14)
ax.set_xlabel("ISO week")
ax.set_ylabel("p95 queue time (ms)")
ax.set_ylim(0, None)
ax.legend(frameon=False, ncol=3, fontsize=7.5, loc="upper center", bbox_to_anchor=(0.5, 1.22))
fig.tight_layout()
plt.show()
```

Outside the incident every region-week clears the 150 ms objective, and the quarter
trends gently downward: that is the caching work that landed in week 27. The canary
penalty is the other visible pattern. Early in the quarter the dashed lines sit well
above their solid counterparts, and by week 39 they have converged. That is the shape
we would expect if the canary build started with a regression that was fixed
incrementally, and it is the claim @sec-model is written to test.

::: {.callout-warning}
## Week 33 is an outage, not a data point

The storage layer in `ap-south` degraded for most of week 33 and the queue backed up
behind it. Those two rows are excluded from the model and from every summary in this
readout, and the exclusion is applied in exactly one place (the `clean` frame in the
setup cell) so it cannot drift between figures. They are kept in @fig-p95 on purpose:
a reader should be able to see what was dropped.
:::

## Is the canary still slower? {#sec-model}

The question is whether the canary channel carries a latency penalty once region and
the quarter-long trend are accounted for. Latency is right-skewed and multiplicative,
so the model is fitted on the log scale, which makes each coefficient a proportional
effect rather than a fixed number of milliseconds:

$$
\log(\text{p95}_{i}) = \beta_0 + \beta_{\text{region}(i)} + \beta_{\text{canary}} \cdot \text{canary}_i + \beta_w \cdot w_i + \varepsilon_i
$$ {#eq-model}

with `eu-west` and `stable` as the reference levels, $w_i$ the ISO week centred on
week 27, and $\varepsilon_i \sim \mathcal{N}(0, \sigma^2)$. This is deliberately the
simplest model that can answer the question; @sec-next says what it does not cover.

```{r}
#| label: fit
#| echo: false
#| include: false

suppressPackageStartupMessages({
  library(readr)
  library(dplyr)
  library(broom)
  library(ggplot2)
  library(knitr)
})

# R graphics get no dual-theme treatment (that is a matplotlib-only hook), so an R
# figure is baked once and has to read on both themes unaided: a mid grey for every
# axis, label and gridline, and the same three data hues the Python figures use.
# (Only the colours are ours: the inline device is already asked for a transparent
# background at kernel start, so making ggplot's own backgrounds transparent below is
# enough to let the page show through.)
options(repr.plot.width = 7, repr.plot.height = 3.1)

INK <- "#7d7d7d"

latency <- read_csv("data/latency.csv", show_col_types = FALSE)

clean <- latency |>
  filter(!(week == 33 & region == "ap-south")) |>
  mutate(
    region  = factor(region, levels = c("eu-west", "us-east", "ap-south")),
    channel = factor(channel, levels = c("stable", "canary")),
    week_c  = week - 27
  )

fit <- lm(log(p95_ms) ~ region + channel + week_c, data = clean)
```

```{r}
#| label: tbl-coefs
#| tbl-cap: "Fitted coefficients on the log scale, with the multiplicative effect each one implies. A factor of 1.00 is no effect."
#| echo: false

# `kable(format = "html")` returns HTML as a *string*. Printing it would show the
# markup as text, so hand it to IRdisplay, which publishes it as a text/html output.
IRdisplay::display_html(as.character(
  tidy(fit, conf.int = TRUE) |>
  mutate(
    term = recode(term,
      "(Intercept)"    = "Intercept (eu-west, stable, week 27)",
      "regionus-east"  = "Region: us-east",
      "regionap-south" = "Region: ap-south",
      "channelcanary"  = "Channel: canary",
      "week_c"         = "Per week elapsed"
    ),
    factor = exp(estimate),
    p.value = if_else(p.value < 0.001, "<0.001", sprintf("%.3f", p.value))
  ) |>
  select(term, estimate, std.error, factor, p.value) |>
  kable(
    digits = c(0, 3, 3, 3, 0),
    col.names = c("Term", "Estimate (log)", "SE", "Factor", "p"),
    format = "html"
  )
))
```

The canary coefficient in @tbl-coefs is the answer. Averaged over the whole quarter the
canary channel is still measurably slower than stable, but the average hides the
convergence @fig-p95 shows: a single channel coefficient is forced to split the
difference between a large early penalty and none at the end. The regional coefficients
behave as @tbl-coverage suggested, with `ap-south` the slowest by a wide margin and
`us-east` a little faster than the `eu-west` reference.

```{r}
#| label: fig-effects
#| fig-cap: "The canary penalty by week, fitted separately in each half of the quarter. The bars are 95% confidence intervals; the dashed rule is no penalty at all."
#| echo: false

by_half <- clean |>
  mutate(half = if_else(week_c <= 5, "Weeks 27-32", "Weeks 33-39")) |>
  group_by(half) |>
  group_modify(~ tidy(lm(log(p95_ms) ~ region + channel + week_c, data = .x),
                      conf.int = TRUE)) |>
  filter(term == "channelcanary") |>
  mutate(across(c(estimate, conf.low, conf.high), exp))

ggplot(by_half, aes(x = half, y = estimate)) +
  geom_hline(yintercept = 1, linetype = "dashed", colour = INK, linewidth = 0.4) +
  geom_errorbar(aes(ymin = conf.low, ymax = conf.high),
                width = 0.12, colour = INK, linewidth = 0.5) +
  geom_point(size = 2.4, colour = "#3b82c4") +
  labs(x = NULL, y = "Canary p95 as a factor of stable") +
  theme_minimal(base_size = 10) +
  theme(
    plot.background  = element_rect(fill = "transparent", colour = NA),
    panel.background = element_rect(fill = "transparent", colour = NA),
    panel.grid.minor = element_blank(),
    panel.grid.major = element_line(colour = INK, linewidth = 0.18),
    text  = element_text(colour = INK),
    axis.text = element_text(colour = INK)
  )
```

Splitting the quarter in half makes the picture honest. @fig-effects fits the same model
separately either side of week 32: the canary penalty is real and large in the first
half, and in the second half its confidence interval covers 1.0, which is to say we can
no longer distinguish the two channels. The regression in the canary build was fixed
during the quarter, and the single pooled coefficient in @tbl-coefs is an artefact of
averaging over that fix.

## What we would do next {#sec-next}

- **Give the model a channel-by-week interaction** rather than fitting the halves
  separately. The split in @fig-effects is a readable stand-in, not the right estimator.
- **Weight by volume.** Every region-week enters the fit equally, so `ap-south` (the
  smallest region by a factor of three) has as much say as `us-east`. That is defensible
  for a latency question and wrong for a user-impact question.
- **Model the error budget too.** This readout only looks at the tail latency half of
  @tbl-slo; the error counts are in the dataset and untouched.

The full data dictionary, the generating process, and the model diagnostics are in
[Methods](methods.tmd).
